# Overview

Semi-targeted peptidomics of endogenous amidated peptides across a mouse tissue
panel (N = 3 per tissue). Peptide identity is confirmed by MS/MS spectral
matching against authentic synthetic standards, using dot product similarity,
precursor mass accuracy and fragment ion count.

Accompanies Wiggenhorn *et al.*, "Global profiling of peptide amidation to
discover bioactive fragments of the secretome."

**To run:** place the input files listed in section 1 in `data/`, or edit
`DATA_DIR`. All outputs are written to `OUT_DIR`.

---

# 1. Configuration and data

```{r load_data}
library(dplyr);  library(tidyr);   library(ggplot2)
library(readr);  library(readxl);  library(stringr)
library(gplots); library(tibble);  library(RColorBrewer)

# ── Paths ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Relative by default so the analysis runs anywhere in the repository.
DATA_DIR <- "data"
OUT_DIR  <- "results"
dir.create(OUT_DIR, showWarnings = FALSE, recursive = TRUE)

PATH_TRANSITIONS <- file.path(DATA_DIR, "Transition_Results.csv")
PATH_BCA         <- file.path(DATA_DIR, "Tissue_Panel_Lysate_BCA.csv")
PATH_QUANT       <- file.path(DATA_DIR, "Peptide_Quantification_merged.csv")
PATH_DETECTED    <- file.path(DATA_DIR, "Detected_Peptides_Only.csv")
PATH_PREV_DET    <- file.path(DATA_DIR, "Mouse_prev_detected.csv")
PATH_PREDICTED   <- file.path(DATA_DIR, "Mouse_predicted.csv")

# ── Peptides excluded before analysis ────────────────────────────────────────
# Ghost integrations: a chromatographic peak was integrated but no interpretable
# MS/MS fragmentation was obtained, so spectral identity could not be assessed.
PEPTIDES_TO_REMOVE <- c(
  "YSNFRKSVDST",   # -
  "RPS",           # Il1rn
  "SHSPALDSDSHT",  # Ccdc80
  "SVADLLGSFE",    # Ccdc80 (second site)
  "GTDFQLNQLE",    # Tf
  "LAP"            # Vwf (second site; the surviving Vwf peptide is PEP-VWF)
)

# ── Detection criteria (Methods) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
DOT_PRODUCT_PRIMARY   <- 0.85  # primary threshold
DOT_PRODUCT_SECONDARY <- 0.70  # relaxed threshold, once a peptide is confirmed
PPM_THRESHOLD         <- 15    # precursor mass accuracy (ppm)
ION_COUNT_PRIMARY     <- 3     # fingerprint ions, primary criterion
ION_COUNT_SECONDARY   <- 2     # fingerprint ions, relaxed criterion
TOP_N_IONS            <- 4     # fingerprint size
MZ_SEPARATION         <- 0.5   # fragment matching tolerance (Da)
DP_DIGITS             <- 2     # dot products are reported, and criteria
                               # applied, at 2 dp — matching the replicate-to-
                               # replicate reproducibility of the measure
                               # (median within-tissue SD ~= 0.13)

# Ion types used for scoring. Restricted to a, b and y ions and their
# ammonia-loss forms, consistent with HCD fragmentation. Ions previously
# designated "z" are reported as y(n)-NH3: the z ion is by definition the y ion
# less ammonia, so these are identical m/z channels under a designation
# appropriate to collisional activation. Channels not assignable to an HCD
# product (x, c) are excluded from scoring.
ION_PATTERN <- "^(a|b|y)[0-9]+(-NH3)?(\\+[0-9])?$"

# Peptides where the standard ion requirement cannot be met for physical
# reasons. TRH is a tripeptide: a1, b1 and y1 fall below the m/z 120
# acquisition floor and y2 gives no signal in the synthetic standard, leaving
# only b2 and a2 observable.
MIN_IONS_OVERRIDE <- c("QHP" = 2)   # keyed by peptide sequence (TRH)

# ── Load ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
df               <- read.csv(PATH_TRANSITIONS)
df_lysate_bca    <- read.csv(PATH_BCA)
df_peptide_quant <- read.csv(PATH_QUANT)
detected_list    <- as.character(read.csv(PATH_DETECTED, header = FALSE)$V1)

cat("Transition rows:", nrow(df),
    "| BCA samples:", nrow(df_lysate_bca),
    "| detected peptides:", length(detected_list), "\n")
```

---

# 2. Precursor mass accuracy

```{r ppm}
precursor_quants <- df %>%
  filter(Fragment.Ion == "precursor", Mass.Error.PPM != "#N/A") %>%
  rename(ppm_error = Mass.Error.PPM) %>%
  mutate(ppm_error = as.numeric(ppm_error)) %>%
  select(ppm_error, Peptide, Replicate)

cat("Precursor ppm entries:", nrow(precursor_quants), "\n")
```

---

# 3. Fragment ion pre-processing

Two transitions whose product m/z fall within the fragment matching tolerance
are the same measured peak at unit resolution. Retaining both would let one peak
contribute twice to a score, so the lower-m/z member is kept and the other
dropped.

```{r preprocess}
df[is.na(df)] <- 0

df <- df %>%
  group_by(Peptide, Replicate) %>%
  arrange(Product.Mz, .by_group = TRUE) %>%
  mutate(mz_diff = Product.Mz - lag(Product.Mz)) %>%
  filter(is.na(mz_diff) | mz_diff >= MZ_SEPARATION) %>%
  select(-mz_diff) %>%
  ungroup()

cat("Rows after de-duplication:", nrow(df), "\n")
```

---

# 4. Fingerprint ions from the 1 µM standard

The most intense qualifying ions in the synthetic standard define a fixed
fingerprint used for all samples. Selection uses the standard alone and happens
**before any tissue data are examined** — this is what makes a high dot product
meaningful. Selecting ions after inspecting tissue data would make the criterion
uninformative, since a large fraction of arbitrary 3–4 ion combinations can be
made to pass for almost any replicate.

```{r top_ions}
top_ions <- df %>%
  filter(Replicate == "1uMStd6",
         !grepl("precursor", Fragment.Ion, ignore.case = TRUE),
         str_detect(Fragment.Ion, ION_PATTERN),      # HCD-consistent ions only
         Area > 0) %>%
  group_by(Peptide) %>%
  arrange(desc(Area), .by_group = TRUE) %>%
  slice_head(n = TOP_N_IONS) %>%
  select(Peptide, Fragment.Ion, Product.Charge, std_area = Area) %>%
  ungroup()

cat("Peptides with a fingerprint:", n_distinct(top_ions$Peptide),
    "| mean ions per peptide:",
    round(nrow(top_ions) / n_distinct(top_ions$Peptide), 2), "\n")
```

---

# 5. Dot product

$$\text{dot product} = \frac{\sum A_i S_i}{\sqrt{\sum A_i^2}\;\sqrt{\sum S_i^2}}$$

$A_i$ = peak area in the sample, $S_i$ = peak area in the standard, summed over
the fixed fingerprint. Both vectors describe the same channels, so the score
reflects agreement in relative intensity. Ion-type labels are descriptive
annotations of matched m/z channels, not inputs to the score.

```{r dot_product}
dot_product_results <- df %>%
  filter(!grepl("precursor", Fragment.Ion, ignore.case = TRUE)) %>%
  inner_join(top_ions, by = c("Peptide", "Fragment.Ion", "Product.Charge")) %>%
  group_by(Peptide, Replicate) %>%
  summarise(
    ion_count   = sum(Area > 0),
    dot_product = round(sum(Area * std_area) /
                        (sqrt(sum(Area^2)) * sqrt(sum(std_area^2))), DP_DIGITS),
    .groups = "drop"
  )

cat("Dot product entries:", nrow(dot_product_results), "\n")
```

---

# 6. Detection criteria

Two tiers. A peptide is **confirmed** if any replicate reaches the primary
criterion; once confirmed, further replicates of that peptide may qualify under
the relaxed criterion. A peptide never confirmed has no detections at all.

- **Primary:** dot product ≥ `r DOT_PRODUCT_PRIMARY`, |ppm| < `r PPM_THRESHOLD`, ≥ `r ION_COUNT_PRIMARY` ions
- **Relaxed:** confirmed peptide, dot product ≥ `r DOT_PRODUCT_SECONDARY`, |ppm| < `r PPM_THRESHOLD`, ≥ `r ION_COUNT_SECONDARY` ions

```{r detection}
results <- dot_product_results %>%
  left_join(precursor_quants, by = c("Peptide", "Replicate")) %>%
  mutate(
    min_ions = ifelse(Peptide %in% names(MIN_IONS_OVERRIDE),
                      MIN_IONS_OVERRIDE[Peptide], ION_COUNT_PRIMARY),
    ppm_ok   = !is.na(ppm_error) & abs(ppm_error) < PPM_THRESHOLD
  ) %>%
  group_by(Peptide) %>%
  mutate(
    tissue_confirmed = any(!grepl("Std", Replicate) & !is.na(dot_product) &
                           dot_product >= DOT_PRODUCT_PRIMARY &
                           ppm_ok & ion_count >= min_ions, na.rm = TRUE),
    is_real_detection = tissue_confirmed & !is.na(dot_product) & ppm_ok &
      ((dot_product >= DOT_PRODUCT_PRIMARY   & ion_count >= min_ions) |
       (dot_product >= DOT_PRODUCT_SECONDARY &
        ion_count >= pmax(ION_COUNT_SECONDARY, min_ions - 1)))
  ) %>%
  ungroup() %>%
  select(-min_ions, -ppm_ok)

cat("Replicate-level results:", nrow(results),
    "| detections:", sum(results$is_real_detection, na.rm = TRUE), "\n")
```

---

# 7. Final peptide panel

```{r panel}
final_panel <- results %>%
  filter(is_real_detection,
         !grepl("Std", Replicate, ignore.case = TRUE),
         !Peptide %in% PEPTIDES_TO_REMOVE,
         Peptide %in% detected_list) %>%
  distinct(Peptide)

cat("Peptides in final panel:", nrow(final_panel), "\n")
```

